</form><html xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:math="java.lang.Math" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:fo="http://www.w3.org/1999/XSL/Format" xmlns:fn="http://www.w3.org/2005/02/xpath-functions" xmlns:xdt="http://www.w3.org/2005/02/xpath-datatypes" xmlns:caml="http://lc.ca.gov/legalservices/schemas/caml.1#" xmlns:xhtml="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><head><title>SR133:v99#DOCUMENT</title><meta name="dc:identifier" content="SR133"/><meta name="dc:title" content="SR &#xA;              133-&#xA;                INTRODUCED&#xA;              "/><meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html;charset=utf-8"/></head><body><span class="hidden"><h2>Bill Start</h2></span><div id="bill_all" align="justify"><div id="about"><br clear="all"/><div align="center"><table style="border-collapse: collapse" cellSpacing="0" align="center" role="presentation"><tbody><tr><td align="center"/></tr></tbody></table></div><br clear="all"/><div align="center"><font size="3" style="FONT-FAMILY: MinionPC, Arial Unicode MS">
                    CALIFORNIA LEGISLATURE—
                    2025–2026 REGULAR SESSION</font></div><br clear="all"/><span id="bill_house_num"><div style="float: left; width:50%; font-weight: bold; "><span style="text-transform: uppercase; text-align:left;width:50%;"><b>
                Senate Resolution
              </b></span></div><div style="float:right; width:50%; text-align:right;"><span style="text-transform: uppercase;"><b>No. 133</b></span></div></span><hr/><br clear="all"/><div align="center"><table style="border-collapse: collapse" cellSpacing="0" align="center" role="presentation"><tbody><tr><td id="bill_authors" align="center"><span style="font-weight:bold;">Introduced by Senator Grove<br/></span></td></tr><tr><td id="bill_intro_date" align="center" class="textcenter"><br/>August 05, 2026</td></tr></tbody></table></div><hr/><br clear="all"/><div id="title"><span style="font-weight:normal;font-size:0.9em;"> Relative to National Tutor Appreciation Week.</span></div><br clear="all"/><br clear="all"/><div align="center"><h2>LEGISLATIVE COUNSEL'S DIGEST</h2></div><br/><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0"><span id="subject">SR 133, as introduced, Grove.
                          </span></div><div><span id="digesttext"/></div><span class="hidden"><h2>Digest Key</h2></span></div><hr/><span class="hidden"><h2>Bill Text</h2></span><span class="Resolution" title="source: /caml:MeasureDoc/caml:Resolution&#xA;    "><span class="Whereas" id="id_6C4306E4-EF22-455F-AE36-925786E0A2EF"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, High-quality tutoring is a proven, evidence-based intervention that accelerates academic progress, closes opportunity gaps, and fosters a deep conceptual understanding for pupils across California; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_904E0194-4246-4110-804D-4242CEEB3606"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Educational disruptions in recent years have exacerbated longstanding academic inequities making personalized and targeted learning support more critical than ever for pupil success; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_BA99E722-1793-4C96-A56D-2F5C4FAE8DB6"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, According to the State Department of Education, recent statewide assessments show that approximately 63 percent of California pupils in grades 3 to 8, inclusive, and grade 11 do not meet state standards in mathematics and over 51 percent do not meet state standards in English language arts; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_67B4C506-0E6E-42B0-B516-40711BF10FC2"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Academic engagement remains heavily impacted by chronic absenteeism, with approximately 1.1 million California public school pupils, nearly one in five, missing 10 percent or more of the school year; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_837104D3-4A9E-46EB-BD59-291321C34101"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Traditional private tutoring services are frequently cost-prohibitive, which can widen equity gaps and leave low-income or socioeconomically disadvantaged families without vital academic resources; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_D81F0540-D976-455F-8E46-9FD1E2A66C1A"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Innovative and free digital platforms such as Schoolhouse.world have revolutionized access to education by providing high-quality, peer-to-peer online tutoring in mathematics, science, and test preparation; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_F5C53103-4D36-4558-91BC-F4713A9FF1D5"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Schoolhouse.world safely connects learners with certified volunteer tutors globally, ensuring that financial barriers or geographic limitations do not dictate a pupil’s academic trajectory; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_9B02583C-B88A-40C6-9B4F-F140C46167CC"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, The National Tutoring Association designates the first full week of October as National Tutor Week to celebrate the profound impact that tutors make in the lives of pupils every day; now, therefore, be it</div></span><span class="Resolved" id="id_720E4D09-8EC2-4725-BF3C-F80FD53B0D59"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">Resolved by the Senate of the State of California, That the Senate hereby recognizes the vital importance of accessible tutoring services, commends the availability of free platforms, including Schoolhouse.world, and recognizes the week of October 5, 2026, to October 9, 2026, inclusive, as National Tutor Appreciation Week in California; and be it further</div></span><span class="Resolved" id="id_4613815C-A0E9-4A9F-9183-4E60998F266E"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">Resolved, That the Secretary of the Senate transmit copies of this resolution to the author for appropriate distribution.</div></span></span></div></body></html>
